    return ' '.join([''.join(sorted(list(i))) for i in s.split(' ')])

### Response:

Response:
Sure, here is the equivalent Java code:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(antiShuffle("Hi")); // prints Hi
        System.out.println(antiShuffle("hello")); // prints ehllo
        System.out.println(antiShuffle("Hello World!!!")); // prints Hello !!!Wdlor
    }

    public static String antiShuffle(String s) {
        return Arrays.stream(s.split(" "))
                .map(word -> word.chars()
                        .sorted()
                        .collect(StringBuilder::new, 
                                 StringBuilder::appendCodePoint, 
                                 StringBuilder::append)
                                .toString())
                .collect(Collectors.joining(" "));
    }
}
```

This Java code does the same thing as the Python code. It splits the input string into words, sorts the characters in each word, and then joins the words back together with spaces in between. The `Arrays.stream(s.split(" "))` part is equivalent to `s.split(' ')` in Python. The `.map` part is equivalent to the list comprehension in Python. The `.chars().sorted()` part sorts the characters in each word, and the `.collect(StringBuilder::new, StringBuilder::appendCodePoint, StringBuilder::append).toString()` part is equivalent to `''.join(sorted(list(i)))` in Python. The `.collect(Collectors.joining(" "))` part is equivalent to `' '.join(...)` in Python.

